{"results":[{"url":"https://nebraska.tv/news/local/commissioners-embarrassed-to-learn-paperwork-issue-could-raise-taxes-on-rental-property-owners","final_url":"https://nebraska.tv/news/local/commissioners-embarrassed-to-learn-paperwork-issue-could-raise-taxes-on-rental-property-owners","title":"Commissioners 'embarrassed' to learn paperwork issue could raise taxes on rental property owners","description":"A technicality could lead to some Grand Island renters paying more. Some rental property owners say they tried to protest higher assessed values on their proper","language":"en","text":"# Commissioners 'embarrassed' to learn paperwork issue could raise taxes on rental property owners\n\n---\n\nby Steve White\n\nTue, August 1, 2023 at 7:29 PM\n\n4VIEW ALL PHOTOS\n\nAUGUST 1, 2023: Hall County Commissioners say they're frustrated some property owners who wanted to protest rising valuations had their paperwork dismissed. (Photo Credit: NTV News)\n\nGRAND ISLAND, Neb. — A technicality could lead to some Grand Island renters paying more. Some rental property owners say they tried to protest higher assessed values on their properties but the paperwork was rejected.\n\n“This is going to hurt people, to the point we don't know what will happen. It's probably going to put people in their cars and homeless,” said Karen Bredthauer, a Hall County Commissioner.\n\nOn 16 duplexes in northwest Grand Island property manager Ray O'Connor says the county lowered the assessed value on four units but on a dozen others the valuation rose $1,127,605. O’Connor said that million dollar jump likely means higher taxes.\n\n“It's about a 24,000 hit in taxes. It's embarrassing to me,” he said of his paperwork mistake.\n\nIt’s a feeling county commissioners share after learning O'Connor protested the rising valuation but missed something on the form, so his protest doesn't count.\n\n“Real candidly we're embarrassed it happened this way we'll try to improve procedures in the future,” County Chairman Ron Peterson said.\n\n“I'm as embarrassed as he mentioned as well,” said fellow commissioner Pam Lancaster.\n\nThree landlords who collectively manage hundreds of rental units came before county commissioners because of concerns.\n\n“They will have to trickle this down to their renters,” Bredthauer said after the meeting.\n\nShe said she attended training about changes to property tax protests but didn't realize the form says if anything is missing the protest is dismissed. And in this case she says that hits renters.\n\n“People live paycheck to paycheck. 80 percent of our population does. So when this hits them, this is food off the table, this is kids not going to games, not going to the movies, this is going to impact families, retired people on fixed incomes because of one technicality,” she said.\n\nThe deputy county assessor says they're doing what the law requires. County Assessor Kristi Wold previously told us people are paying top dollar to buy homes. Nebraska law requires valuations to consider sales when setting valuations so Wold said market forces are responsible for rising valuations.\n\nWold’s deputy, Mandy Pfeil told commissioners, “We did our jobs and everybody's making it seem like we're these horrible, horrible people. We just do what we have to do.”\n\nWhat no one in the county did is something they may do next year and notify those who missed something in the paperwork, giving them a chance to have their protest heard.\n\nPeterson said he doesn’t fault Wold and the assessor’s office. He said they are following guidelines.\n\nDuring the meeting commissioners said protests are given to the county clerk’s office. With more than 1,700 protests this year commissioners said it would have been difficult to screen forms to see which had missing information.\n\nWith the prospect that taxes could rise Commissioner Bredthauer proposes using extra funds as tax relief. She wants to take more than $1.5 million from the county’s inheritance tax fund and use that but she said the idea doesn't seem to be gaining traction.","author":"Steve White","published_date":"2023-08-02T00:29:38.000Z","latency_ms":1403.4410169988405,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}